<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mixtón War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1540">1540</a>–<a href="/years/1542">1542</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mixtón War was a rising of the Caxcan and other indigenous peoples of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> against Spanish rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-galicia">New Galicia</a> from 1540 to 1542. The rebels, fortified on mountain strongholds such as the Peñol of Mixtón, beat back early Spanish attacks, and the conquistador <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Pedro de Alvarado</a> died of injuries received in the fighting. Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-mendoza">Antonio de Mendoza</a> crushed the revolt with a large army of Spaniards and indigenous allies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mixton-war.jpg" alt="Mixtón War" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jaontiveros · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xochipilla.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caxcanes">Caxcanes</a>, the heart of the rising, lived in what is now northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalisco">Jalisco</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aguascalientes">Aguascalientes</a>. Though the Spaniards counted them among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichimeca">Chichimeca</a>, they seem to have been settled farmers living in permanent towns, and probably spoke a Uto-Aztecan language; the Zacatecos also joined the revolt. Their first contact with the Spanish came in 1529, when Nuño Beltrán de Guzmán marched north from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> with 300-400 Spaniards and 5,000 to 8,000 Aztec and Tlaxcalan allies. Over six years Guzmán killed, tortured and enslaved thousands, founded the province of Nueva Galicia with towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalajara-mexico">Guadalajara</a>, and forced natives into servitude under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a> system.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The rising began in spring 1540, perhaps encouraged by the departure of Governor Francisco Vásquez de Coronado with more than 1,600 Spaniards and allies on his expedition north. Its spark seems to have been the arrest of eighteen native leaders and the hanging of nine. The rebels killed the encomendero Juan de Arze and two priests, and many fled to the hill fortress of Mixtón, where they repelled an assault by the acting governor Cristóbal de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onati">Oñate</a>. Their most prominent leader was Tenamaztle of Nochistlán.</p><p>In June 1541 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Pedro de Alvarado</a> attacked Mixtón with 400 Spaniards without waiting for reinforcements and was beaten back by an estimated 15,000 defenders under Tenamaztle and Don Diego, a Zacateco; on 24 June a horse fell on him, and he died on 4 July. The rebels attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalajara-mexico">Guadalajara</a> in September but were repulsed. Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-mendoza">Antonio de Mendoza</a> then invaded with 450 Spaniards and 30,000-60,000 Aztec, Tlaxcalan and other allies, took the strongholds one by one in a war of no quarter, captured Nochistlán on 9 November 1541, and took Mixtón early in 1542.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Thousands of the defeated were sent in chains to the mines, and many survivors, mostly women and children, were moved to work on Spanish farms. On the viceroy's order many prisoners were executed, some by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> fire, some torn apart by dogs, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-indies">Council of the Indies</a> opened a secret inquiry into his conduct. The victory gave the Spanish the region of Guadalajara and access to the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> deposits, and Spanish expansion led on to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichimeca-war">Chichimeca War</a> of 1550-1590. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caxcanes">Caxcanes</a> were absorbed into Spanish society, though fiestas of the Tastuanes in Moyahua de Estrada and Apozol still commemorate the war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mixtón War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>Mixtón War</b> of 1540–1542 the Caxcanes and other peoples of New Galicia rose against Spanish rule, and Viceroy Mendoza himself led the army that crushed them with thousands of indigenous auxiliaries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Tlaxcalan troops went on to fight in Spanish campaigns across Mesoamerica, including the conquest of Guatemala led by Pedro de Alvarado in the 1520s and the <b>Mixtón War</b> of 1540–1542.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tlaxcala">Tlaxcala · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Mixtón War</b> of 1540-42, a major indigenous uprising, nearly ended Spanish rule there.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-new-galicia">Kingdom of New Galicia</a></li><li><blockquote>Their mountain stronghold, centred on the Mesa del Nayar, became a refuge for groups fleeing colonial rule, especially after the <b>Mixtón War</b> of 1540-42.</blockquote><a href="/state/nayar">Nayar</a></li></ul></section>
